  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041851/assets/cb931a29b19302801d6586c37b4d2282e37776aa58b761f1fcb088a8fa19f9ff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=1.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=7?$",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=1,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=1+4=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=5.$",
